# @boundary runs
# @boundary-pending BUG-232  argument-count checking is skipped inside ${...}
#
# PINS A BUG, NOT A FEATURE — and this is the one worth reading.
#
# `add` takes two arguments. Called inside a `${...}` interpolation with one, the
# compiler says NOTHING and the program runs, printing 1 (the missing argument is
# padded with a deterministic zero, per BUG-142's partial fix). The IDENTICAL call
# written as a statement emits `warning: too few arguments to 'add': expected 2,
# found 1` — see bv_arity_too_few.zbr, which is the control.
#
# The same asymmetry holds for too MANY arguments: extra arguments are silently
# discarded inside an interpolation and warned about outside it.
#
# The .expected file below therefore records SILENTLY WRONG OUTPUT on purpose. It
# is the current answer, not the right one. When BUG-232 is fixed this probe will
# fail — rewrite it as `# @boundary warns too few arguments` at that point.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE OTHER TWO: `print("${f(...)}")` is the most common
# shape in the language, so this is the context where a user is MOST likely to
# make an arity mistake and LEAST likely to be told. Found by the A3 boundary
# suite, 2026-07-30; both compilers behave identically.
def add(a: int, b: int): int
    return a + b

def one(a: int): int
    return a

def main()
    print("tooFew=${add(1)}")
    print("tooMany=${one(1, 2)}")
